+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,123 @@
+## Deploying Fizzy
+
+Fizzy ships with a [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org/) configuration for running
+the whole app on a single Linux server: the web server, the background job
+worker, the SQLite databases and every uploaded file all live in **one
+container**, behind `kamal-proxy`, which terminates TLS for you.
+
+Everything you need is declared in [`config/deploy.yml`](../config/deploy.yml).
+To stand up your own copy you fill in three things — your hostname, your mail
+server, and your secrets — and then run one command.
+
+### Requirements
+
+- A Linux server with Docker installed and SSH access. 1 GB of RAM is enough to
+  start; give it more if you expect real usage.
+- A domain (or subdomain) whose DNS `A`/`AAAA` record points at that server.
+- Ports 80 and 443 open on the server, so `kamal-proxy` can obtain a Let's
+  Encrypt certificate and serve HTTPS.
+- Docker on the machine you deploy from, since Kamal builds the image there.
+
+### 1. Point your hostname at the server
+
+Create a DNS record for the hostname you want to use, for example
+`fizzy.example.com`, and point it at your server's IP address.
+
+### 2. Configure the deployment
+
+Open [`config/deploy.yml`](../config/deploy.yml) and edit:
+
+- `servers.web` — replace `192.168.0.1` with your server's IP address or
+  hostname. This is the SSH target Kamal deploys to.
+- `proxy.host` — replace `fizzy.example.com` with the hostname from step 1.
+- `env.clear.APP_FQDN` — the same hostname. Fizzy uses it to build absolute
+  links in emails and notifications.
+- `env.clear.SMTP_ADDRESS` (and, if needed, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_USERNAME`,
+  `SMTP_DOMAIN`, `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION`, `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS`) — your mail
+  server. This is how Fizzy sends the magic-link sign-in emails.
+- `env.clear.MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` — the address those emails come from.
+- `builder.arch` — set to `arm64` if your server is ARM based.
+
+The default `registry` block runs a local registry and tunnels it to the
+server, so you do not need a Docker Hub/GHCR account to get started. If you
+would rather push to a registry, set `registry.server` and `registry.username`,
+uncomment the `registry.password` entry, and give `image` a name that includes
+your username.
+
+### 3. Provide your secrets
+
+Secrets live in [`.kamal/secrets`](../.kamal/secrets). That file is safe to
+commit because it only refers to environment variables and commands; the values
+stay on your machine. At minimum you need to export:
+
+```sh
+# A secret key base for Rails. Keep this stable across deploys.
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE=$(bin/rails secret)
+
+# The password for your mail server, if it requires authentication.
+export SMTP_PASSWORD=...
+```
+
+If you configured a remote registry, also export the access token you set up in
+step 2:
+
+```sh
+export KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=...
+```
+
+### 4. Deploy
+
+From the repository root:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+`kamal setup` builds the image, pushes it, starts `kamal-proxy`, boots the app
+and prepares the databases. Once it finishes, Fizzy is available at
+`https://your-hostname`. For later changes, just run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+### Where your data lives
+
+Both the SQLite databases and Active Storage uploads live under
+`/rails/storage` in the container. `config/deploy.yml` mounts a named Docker
+volume, `fizzy_storage`, there, so the data is kept across deploys and
+container restarts.
+
+To back it up, copy the files out of the volume:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app exec --interactive --reuse "tar czf - -C /rails/storage ." > fizzy-backup.tar.gz
+```
+
+### Background jobs
+
+Fizzy uses [Solid Queue](https://github.com/rails/solid_queue) for background
+jobs and runs it **inside Puma** (see `config/puma.rb`). There is no Redis and
+no separate worker container: the single web container processes jobs too.
+
+### Email
+
+Fizzy signs people in with magic links, so a working mail server matters. The
+`SMTP_*` variables under `env.clear` in `config/deploy.yml` point at your mail
+server, and `config/environments/production.rb` turns them into Action Mailer
+settings. If your provider only gives you a URL, most support an SMTP endpoint
+that maps onto the same settings.
+
+### Common commands
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy                 # deploy the latest code
+bin/kamal app logs -f            # tail the application logs
+bin/kamal console                # open a Rails console on the server
+bin/kamal shell                  # open a shell in the container
+bin/kamal dbc                    # open the SQLite console
+bin/kamal app exec "bin/rails db:migrate"   # run migrations
+```
+
+Set `RAILS_LOG_LEVEL=debug` in `config/deploy.yml` if you need more detail when
+something goes wrong.
